.TH SYSCTL 2 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sysctl \- read or write kernel state via a MIB path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysctl.h>
.PP
.BI "int sysctl(int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ,
.BI "           void *" oldp ", size_t *" oldlenp ,
.BI "           void *" newp ", size_t " newlen ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sysctl ()
retrieves or modifies kernel parameters.
Parameters are organised as a tree and are addressed by Management
Information Base (MIB) style integer arrays.
.PP
.I name
is an array of
.I namelen
integers designating the parameter.
.I namelen
must be in the range
.RB [2,\~ CTL_MAXNAME ].
.PP
.SS Reading a value
If
.I oldp
is non-NULL, the kernel copies the current value of the named parameter
into the buffer it points to.
.I oldlenp
must point to a
.I size_t
that on entry holds the capacity of the buffer, and on return receives
the number of bytes actually written.
.PP
To probe the required buffer size without reading the value, pass
.B NULL
for
.I oldp
and a non-NULL
.IR oldlenp
initialised to zero.
The kernel will set
.I *oldlenp
to the current size of the parameter.
This is the idiom used by the
.BR sysctl (3)
libc helpers to implement retry loops:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
size_t sz = 0;
sysctl(mib, miblen, NULL, &sz, NULL, 0);   /* probe */
buf = malloc(sz);
sysctl(mib, miblen, buf, &sz, NULL, 0);    /* fetch */
.EE
.RE
.PP
If
.I oldlenp
is NULL and
.I oldp
is NULL, the kernel ignores the read side entirely.
.PP
.SS Writing a value
If
.I newp
is non-NULL, the kernel copies
.I newlen
bytes from
.I newp
into the parameter.
Writing requires
.B CTLFLAG_WR
to be set on the parameter and the caller to have effective user-ID 0
(root).
Reading and writing may be combined in a single call: the old value
is fetched before the new value is installed.
.PP
.SS Atomicity
Reads and writes of a single parameter are serialised by an internal
.I sysctl_mutex
held for the duration of the handler call, unless the parameter is
registered with
.BR CTLFLAG_NOLOCK .
Callers must not assume atomicity across multiple
.BR sysctl ()
calls.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.
On failure, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I name ,
.IR oldp ,
.IR oldlenp ,
or
.I newp
points to an invalid address.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I namelen
is less than 2 or greater than
.BR CTL_MAXNAME ;
or the handler rejected the supplied
.I newlen
as the wrong size for a typed parameter.
.TP
.B ENAMETOOLONG
The new string value is longer than the maximum length allowed for
that parameter.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The buffer supplied via
.I oldp
is smaller than the current value of the parameter.
.I *oldlenp
is updated to the required size so the caller can retry with a larger
buffer.
This error is used by
.BR sysctl (3)
to drive its retry loop.
.TP
.B ENOENT
The MIB path does not correspond to any registered parameter.
.TP
.B EPERM
.I newp
is non-NULL but the parameter is read-only
.RB ( CTLFLAG_WR
not set), or the caller is not root.
.SH NOTES
.BR sysctl (3)
provides typed convenience wrappers
.RB ( sysctl_int (),
.BR sysctl_string (),
\&…)
that handle size probing, buffer growth, and
.B ENOMEM
retry loops automatically.
Prefer those wrappers for application code; use the raw syscall only
when a wrapper's overhead is unacceptable or when implementing the
wrappers themselves.
.PP
The
.B sysctl
tree can be explored by name using
.BR sysctlbyname (3)
and
.BR sysctlnametomib (3).
.SH CONFORMING TO
This interface first appeared in 4.4BSD and is present on BSD-derived
systems including FreeBSD and Substrate.
It is not part of POSIX.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sysctl (3),
.BR sysctlbyname (3),
.BR sysctlnametomib (3),
.BR sysctl (8)
.SH COLOPHON
This page is part of the Substrate operating system documentation.
